\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
For $3\leq n\leq 9$, let u be smooth on a nonempty open convex $\Omega \subset R^{n}$ with $D^{2}u$ positive definite and $\Sigma _ij$ $U^ij$ $\partial _ij(det$ $D^{2}u)^(-(n+1)/(n+2))=0$, $U=det(D^{2}u)(D^{2}u)^{-1}$. If the graph metric $\delta _ij+u_i$ $u_j$ is complete, must $\Omega =R^{n}$ and $u(x)=\tfrac12 x^{T}Ax+b\cdot x+c$ with A positive definite?

\subsection{Short English statement}
For $3\leq n\leq 9$, let u be smooth on a nonempty open convex $\Omega \subset R^{n}$ with $D^{2}u$ positive definite and $\Sigma _ij$ $U^ij$ $\partial _ij(det$ $D^{2}u)^(-(n+1)/(n+2))=0$, $U=det(D^{2}u)(D^{2}u)^{-1}$. If the graph metric $\delta _ij+u_i$ $u_j$ is complete, must $\Omega =R^{n}$ and $u(x)=\tfrac12 x^{T}Ax+b\cdot x+c$ with A positive definite?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{itemize}
\item[S1] OpenAI, manuscript citation page. Source of the formulation motivating this local problem.
\url{https://github.com/openai/math/blob/adc7f1241b42e322a6451854ab7e4b4c146bf78a/preprints/The-affine-Bernstein-theorem-in-dimensions-three-through-nine-September-24-2026/README.md}.
\end{itemize}

